class SettingsPreset(Choice):
    """
    Settings preset.

    Selecting a preset will replace all randomizer settings to make them follow the selected preset, which can
    either be a randomizer built-in preset or a custom one. If you have made unsaved changes to your randomizer
    settings, be sure to save them before selecting a preset, in order to avoid losing them.
    Built-in randomizer presets currently include the following:
    - Default: the default settings for the randomizer, including Basic skill assumptions and Normal item
        progression. This is highly recommended for first-time players.
    - Community Racing Season 3: the settings currently used in community races, including Hard skill assumptions,
        Tricky item progression with 2 additional Super packs, and "Save the animals" required.
    To create a new custom preset, use the "Save Settings" button at the bottom of the page. To manage existing
    custom presets, click the gear button to the right of the preset selection.

    The settings preset is the base for all settings: the category presets and individual settings below are
    applied on top of it, wherever they are set to something other than 'preset'.
    """
    display_name = "Settings preset"
    option_default = 0
    option_community_race_season_5 = 1
    option_mentor_tournament = 2
    option_summer_series_expert_challenge = 3
    default = 0
    preset_names = {0: 'Default', 1: 'Community Race Season 5', 2: 'Mentor Tournament', 3: 'Summer Series Expert Challenge'}


class SkillAssumptionsPreset(PresetChoice):
    """
    Skill assumptions.

    Skill assumptions determine which tricks the randomizer assumes the player is able to perform. This affects
    where the randomizer may place key items on any given step. Anything selected here is "fair game" for the
    player to be required to do in order to progress.
    After selecting one of the preset difficulty levels, it is possible to refine it by clicking the gear button
    on the right and modifying the selections. Note that if you then select a preset again, it will override any
    customized settings that you made.
    The descriptions below are only a brief summary. Clicking the gear button will show a list of all the tricks
    in detail, with animations. It's recommended to take a look to make sure you understand all the kinds of
    things that you may be expected to be able to do!
    Examples of expected techniques from the skill presets are as follows:
    - Basic: Wall jumps, shinesparks, simple uses of frozen enemies, and navigating heated rooms without Varia
        (but only with plenty of extra energy).
    - Medium: 1-tap shortcharge, short infinite bomb jumps, sequences of 3 or more wall jumps, and underwater
        movement tech such as crouch jumps, down grabs, mid-air Spring Ball jumps, and Gravity jumps.
    - Hard: 2-tap shortcharge, horizontal mid-air shinesparks, mockballs, gate glitching, trickier climbing on
        enemies, ice clips assisted by Morph and X-Ray, cross-room jumps from air to water rooms, and using a
        turn-around to jump around a corner.
    - Very Hard: Stutter 3-tap, complex shinespark setups, stutter water shortcharges, speedballs, temporary blue
        suit, horizontal damage boosts, Spring Ball jumps off of walls, Flatley jumps, evading Metroids, short
        X-ray climbs, CWJ with retries.
    - Expert: Stutter 4-tap, double Spring Ball jumps, precise ice clips, temporary blue chains, pause abuse,
        grapple jumps, spring flings, and very precise jumps that get extra height with Speed Booster.
    - Expert+: Bypassing locked door shells, glitched X-ray modes (G-mode, R-mode, X-mode), blue suits (Crystal
        Spark, R-mode spark interrupt), and flash suits (spikesuit, R-mode CF interrupt).
    - Extreme: Double-stutter 4-tap, underwater wall jumps, moonfalls between frozen enemies, X-mode blue suits,
        R-mode standup clips, and complex G-mode applications (such as upward setups and artificial morph).
    - Extreme+: Moondances, bootless double Spring Ball jumps underwater with Bombs, diagonal bomb jumps,
        utilizing the initial spark when gaining a flash suit, back-to-back X-mode to gain a blue suit, and G-mode
        into and from heat.
    - Insane: Triple-stutter 4-tap, Taco Tank, elevator Crystal Flash, slope sparks, downward G-mode setups,
        moondances that require exact counts, and very slow and unforgiving strats.
    - Insane+: Crossing Colosseum with no movement items, ceiling bomb jumps, free fall clips, bootless UWJ within
        a two-tile wide gap, super sinks, first-try spike suits, and R-mode knockback sparks.

    The preset for this category of settings, as on the maprando.com website. 'preset' keeps the category from the
    selected settings preset (settings_preset). Individual settings of the category can still be changed with the
    options below.
    Implicit and Beyond are not offered on the website, but are valid Map Rando presets.
    """
    display_name = "Skill assumptions"
    path = "skill_assumption_settings.preset"
    preset_dir = "skill-assumptions"
    option_implicit = 1
    option_basic = 2
    option_medium = 3
    option_hard = 4
    option_very_hard = 5
    option_expert = 6
    option_expert_plus = 7
    option_extreme = 8
    option_extreme_plus = 9
    option_insane = 10
    option_insane_plus = 11
    option_beyond = 12
    json_values = {1: 'Implicit', 2: 'Basic', 3: 'Medium', 4: 'Hard', 5: 'Very Hard', 6: 'Expert', 7: 'Expert+', 8: 'Extreme', 9: 'Extreme+', 10: 'Insane', 11: 'Insane+', 12: 'Beyond'}


class ItemProgressionPreset(PresetChoice):
    """
    Item progression.

    Item progression settings affect the rate at which the randomizer will place key items, and which locations it
    will place them in, within the constraints of the selected "Skill assumptions" above. Four presets are
    available:
    - Normal: Key items are placed generously: by the time 50% of items are obtainable, at least 75% of key items
        should be obtainable. Morph, Bombs, and an ETank are placed early. Key items have no specific tendency to
        be in harder or easier locations to reach. Beams, additional tanks, and ammo are spread out uniformly.
        Multiple progression options are generally possible from relatively early in the game.
    - Tricky: Key items are spread out at a roughly uniform pace but tend to be placed in harder-to-reach
        locations. Beams, tanks, and ammo are spread out uniformly as filler. Multiple progression options are
        generally possible by the middle of the game.
    - Technical: Key items are delayed and tend to be placed in harder-to-reach locations. Tanks and certain beams
        (Charge, Wave, Spazer) are spread out as filler. Access to ammo other than Missiles is often delayed. A
        single progression sequence may be forced until late in the game.
    - Challenge: Key items are delayed similarly to "Technical", but access to tanks is often delayed. Charge and
        Spazer are the only beams placed as filler. A single progression sequence may be forced until late in the
        game.
    - Desolate: Some key items may not be placed at all, and the amount of ammo and tanks is greatly reduced. A
        single progression sequence may be forced through almost the entire game.
    For players new to this randomizer, the "Normal" preset is highly recommended. The "Tricky" preset and higher
    settings tend to make the game significantly more difficult. The "Technical" setting is recommended for
    players who want to be forced to learn new techniques to progress. Be careful selecting "Challenge" or
    "Desolate" settings as these can be fairly punishing: they are likely to require the player to go without
    Energy Tanks for much of the game.
    "Item progression" and "Skill assumptions" synergize with one another: harder item progression settings
    increase the likelihood that harder techniques selected in the "Skill assumptions" will actually be required
    to beat the game.
    The item progression settings can be customized by clicking the gear button on the right. Note that selecting
    a preset again will override any customized selections.

    The preset for this category of settings, as on the maprando.com website. 'preset' keeps the category from the
    selected settings preset (settings_preset). Individual settings of the category can still be changed with the
    options below.
    """
    display_name = "Item progression"
    path = "item_progression_settings.preset"
    preset_dir = "item-progression"
    option_normal = 1
    option_tricky = 2
    option_technical = 3
    option_challenge = 4
    option_desolate = 5
    json_values = {1: 'Normal', 2: 'Tricky', 3: 'Technical', 4: 'Challenge', 5: 'Desolate'}


class QualityOfLifePreset(PresetChoice):
    """
    Quality-of-life options.

    These options help provide a smoother, more intuitive, and less tedious game experience. Several presets are
    provided:
    - Off: All quality-of-life options are turned off. This is for players who want a full experience of
        exploration, with a similar feel to the original game. This may involve a lot of wandering around and
        backtracking to figure out where to go. Map stations are harder to find and provide minimal assistance as
        all item dots look the same.
    - Low: Many quality-of-life options are turned off, restoring some unintuitive but potentially interesting
        vanilla game behavior: some items do not spawn until defeating Phantoon or waking the planet, Acid Chozo
        statue requires Space Jump to use it, and completing the escape may require collecting certain items
        and/or carefully managing energy in the Mother Brain fight. Map stations are somewhat less powerful
        because all ammo and tank items appear as small dots the same as Missiles.
    - Default: Quality-of-life options are turned to their generally recommended settings (mostly on). Map
        stations are very useful, with different item dot shapes to distinguish three tiers of items: Missiles,
        other ammo/tank items, and unique items. Enemy drops are somewhat improved, reducing the need for long
        farms.
    - High: Map stations reveal even more information: ammo items (Supers and Power Bombs) are shown with a a
        small "X" while Energy Tanks and Reserve Tanks are shown with an "O". Map stations also reveal area
        connections in neighboring areas, showing how they connect back to the current area. Energy stations and
        E-Tanks also refill reserve energy, and E-Tanks can be disabled.
    - Max: All quality-of-life options are turned on to their highest settings. This includes respin, lenient
        Space Jump, and momentum conservation to more fluidly control Samus, and an extra-fast Mother Brain fight
        that completely skips the second and third phases. Energy can also be manually transferred back into
        reserve tanks.
    Individual options may be customized by clicking the gear button on the right.

    The preset for this category of settings, as on the maprando.com website. 'preset' keeps the category from the
    selected settings preset (settings_preset). Individual settings of the category can still be changed with the
    options below.
    """
    display_name = "Quality-of-life options"
    path = "quality_of_life_settings.preset"
    preset_dir = "quality-of-life"
    option_off = 1
    option_low = 2
    option_default = 3
    option_high = 4
    option_max = 5
    json_values = {1: 'Off', 2: 'Low', 3: 'Default', 4: 'High', 5: 'Max'}


class ObjectivesPreset(PresetChoice):
    """
    Objectives.

    This setting determines the conditions needed to open the way to Mother Brain:
    - None: The way is already open from the start of the game.
    - Bosses: Defeat Kraid, Phantoon, Draygon, and Ridley.
    - Minibosses: Defeat Spore Spawn, Crocomire, Botwoon, and Golden Torizo.
    - Metroids: Defeat all the Metroids in the four Metroid rooms.
    - Chozos: Defeat Bomb Torizo and Golden Torizo, and activate Bowling Alley and Acid Chozo statues. Note that
        Phantoon must be defeated before the Bowling Alley statue can be activated.
    - Pirates: Defeat the enemies in Pit Room, Baby Kraid Room, Plasma Room, and Metal Pirates Room. Note that
        Morph and Missiles must be collected before the enemies spawn in Pit Room.
    - Random: Complete 4 random objectives from the above list. Search for X's on the map to find the objectives
        to complete.
    In every case, the way to beat the game is to escape after defeating Mother Brain. Objectives are marked with
    X's on the map.

    The preset for this category of settings, as on the maprando.com website. 'preset' keeps the category from the
    selected settings preset (settings_preset). Individual settings of the category can still be changed with the
    options below.
    """
    display_name = "Objectives"
    path = "objective_settings.preset"
    preset_dir = "objectives"
    option_none = 1
    option_bosses = 2
    option_minibosses = 3
    option_chozos = 4
    option_pirates = 5
    option_metroids = 6
    option_randomized = 7
    json_values = {1: 'None', 2: 'Bosses', 3: 'Minibosses', 4: 'Chozos', 5: 'Pirates', 6: 'Metroids', 7: 'Random'}


class DoorsPreset(PresetChoice):
    """
    Doors.

    This setting determines the types of non-gray doors that exist in the game:
    - Blue: All doors are blue, except for gray doors.
    - Ammo: Red, green, and yellow doors are randomly mixed in.
    - Beam: Beam doors are randomly mixed in, replacing some of the ammo doors.
    Gray doors are unaffected by this setting and always exist in the same set of rooms: boss/miniboss rooms and
    the four Pirates rooms (Pit Room, Baby Kraid Room, Plasma Room, and Metal Pirates Room).
    For ammo and beam doors, both sides of the door will have the same color and share a lock, so that unlocking
    one side of the door also unlocks the other.
    Ammo and beam doors will not be placed on or immediately connect to any special tile such as a save, map,
    refill, objective "X", or a gray door.
    Click on the gear icon to customize the number of ammo and beam doors that can appear. This sets the maximum
    number of doors, and the randomizer will attempt to place that many. In certain cases, fewer doors than the
    maximum can be placed.

    The preset for this category of settings, as on the maprando.com website. 'preset' keeps the category from the
    selected settings preset (settings_preset). Individual settings of the category can still be changed with the
    options below.
    """
    display_name = "Doors"
    path = "doors_settings.preset"
    preset_dir = "doors"
    option_blue = 1
    option_ammo = 2
    option_beam = 3
    json_values = {1: 'Blue', 2: 'Ammo', 3: 'Beam'}


